{
  "schema": "opentallas.architecture.abi3_control_path_breakdown.v1",
  "question": "On the ABI 3.0 RTL path, where do the reduced Qwen3 token's cycles go -- control (sequencer, admission, issue, engine-internal walk control) or datapath -- and which blocks set the clock?",
  "answer": "Throughput: 12,962,353 profiled cycles for the ROM decode token, of which only 5,984 (0.046%) are spent outside an engine -- 72 cycles per issue across 83 issues in the microsequencer front end, the completion adapter and the admission bridge. The control overhead that matters is INSIDE an engine: DMA.SCATTER took 6,341,464 cycles (48.9%) to append eight one-row KV entries, because the index mover republished the whole 8,256-row prior plane into itself at three cycles per word. The correctly rounded exponential behind VECTOR.SILU_MUL and ATTENTION.GQA takes another 5,269,625 (40.7%) and is serial datapath arithmetic, not control; the MAC lane is active 1,277,952 cycles (9.9%) at 98.4% efficiency inside its launches. The \"six cycles of control per MAC\" ratio is therefore half an engine-internal walk (a control fix) and half a serial transcendental (a datapath fix). Latency: an issue reaches its engine 9 + 33 cycles after the sequencer issues it and the next bridge accept follows 36 cycles after the previous completion -- negligible against engine runs of 46 to 1.1 M cycles. Clock: the slowest measured vehicle blocks are datapath (ot_a3_qwen_gqa 69.7 MHz not met, fp32_div_rne 283.5, the transcendental 306.8). The control block quoted as a limiter, the full 12-module microsequencer, was recorded at 74.9 MHz not met WITH the state controller at the 2026-09-19 sources (worst path: the state controller's combinational modulo) and 317.1 MHz closed without it; re-routed here at the current sources it closes at 3.0 ns, 397.5 MHz with the state controller (478,193 cells), above the divider, the transcendental and the GQA engine it issues to -- the sequencer is not the clock limiter today. The admission bridge, device top and engine array have never been routed on their own.",
